{"name":"Diamonds in the Sky","hex":"#e5e2e1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diamonds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of diamond"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♦.","The testicles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diamonds"},{"term":"sky","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Sikaiana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The atmosphere above a given point, especially as visible from the surface of the Earth as the place where the sun, moon, stars, and clouds are seen.","With a descriptive word: the part of the sky which can be seen from a specific place or at a specific time; its climate, condition, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Diamonds in the Sky","blocks":[{"text":"Diamonds in the Sky is a 2018 Nigerian drama film directed by Kunle Afolayan and produced by Femi Adebayo. The film stars Femi Adebayo, Omowumi Dada, and Joke Silva with Ali Nuhu, Bolaji Oba, and Toyin Abraham in supporting roles. The film tells the story of three families that struggle when cancer affects their lives."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b8/Diamonds_In_The_Sky_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamonds_in_the_Sky"},{"title":"Diamond","blocks":[{"text":"Diamond is a mineral form of the element carbon with its atoms arranged in a crystal structure called diamond cubic. Diamond is a tasteless, odorless, strong, brittle solid, a poor conductor of electricity, colorless in pure form, and insoluble in water. Another solid form of carbon known as graphite is the chemically stable form of carbon at room temperature and pressure, but diamond is metastable and converts to it at a negligible rate under those conditions. Diamond has the highest hardness and thermal conductivity of any natural material, properties that are used in major industrial applications such as cutting and polishing tools."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rough_Diamond.jpg/330px-Rough_Diamond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond"},{"title":"Sky","blocks":[{"text":"The sky is an unobstructed view upward from the surface of the Earth. It includes the atmosphere and outer space. It may also be considered a place between the ground and outer space, thus distinct from outer space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg/330px-July_night_sky_%2835972569256%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky"},{"title":"Sky blue","blocks":[{"text":"Sky blue refers to a collection of shades comparable to that of a clear daytime sky. Typically it is a shade of cyan or light teal, though some iterations are closer to light azure or light blue. The term is attested from 1681. A 1585 translation of Nicolas de Nicolay's 1576 Les navigations, peregrinations et voyages faicts en la Turquie includes \"the tulbant [turban] of the merchant must be skie coloured\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Shades_of_light_blue.png/330px-Shades_of_light_blue.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/500px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/1280px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Diamant_gisements.jpg/500px-Diamant_gisements.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Main diamond producing countries (in French)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiamant_gisements.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Diamond_structure.gif/500px-Diamond_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Diamond_structure.gif/500px-Diamond_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diamond unit cell, showing the tetrahedral structure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiamond_structure.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg/500px-Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg/1280px-Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg/500px-Diamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One face of an uncut octahedral diamond, showing trigons (of positive and negative relief) formed by natural chemical etching","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiamond_face_trigons_scale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Vickers_anvil_diamons.jpg/500px-Vickers_anvil_diamons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Vickers_anvil_diamons.jpg/1280px-Vickers_anvil_diamons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikim